{
  "biological_interpretation": "Retromer core loss has a selective thalamic synaptic/excitability transcriptional output that is distinct from simple transcriptional replacement of the retromer, sorting nexin, WASH, or TBC1D5 machinery.",
  "caveats": [
    "The exact response is cell-type-selective and cannot be generalized to all neurons.",
    "The assay measured total mRNA and did not test retromer cargo localization or endosomal trafficking directly."
  ],
  "cell_types": [
    "151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut",
    "110 CNU-HYa HY MM Glut"
  ],
  "exact_observed_result": "Vps35 perturbation in 151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut produced FDR-supported changes in a synaptic and excitability module including Grin2b, Rims3, L1cam, Kcnc2, Ache, Cttnbp2, Lrrc7, Ppp1r9b, and Dgkz. Strong Vps35 self-knockdown in 110 CNU-HYa HY MM Glut stayed target-only, and 151 TH did not significantly compensate Vps26a, Vps26b, Vps29, Snx1, Snx6, Tbc1d5, or Washc2 mRNAs.",
  "preliminary_scores": {
    "novelty": 4,
    "priority": 4,
    "support": 5
  },
  "prior_expectation": "VPS35 is a retromer core subunit implicated in neuronal endosomal trafficking and neurodegenerative disease; a synaptic-cargo RNA output is biologically plausible but not a direct measure of cargo sorting.",
